"""A Level French (0745), Lower Sixth: Grammaire Avancée et Stylistique Comparée; Méthodologie de la Traduction : Thème et Version
(10 lessons, one practice set each in the practice file).

Explanations are in English for Anglophone learners; examples, exercises and model answers are in French.
All example sentences are original and invented. Where textbooks differ the lesson says 'check your textbook'.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("french", "lower-sixth")

M1 = "Grammaire Avancée et Stylistique Comparée"
M2 = "Méthodologie de la Traduction : Thème et Version"

LESSONS = []

# ================================================================== 1. Le subjonctif
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Le subjonctif",
    title="Le subjonctif : quand l'employer et comment le former",
    slug="frl1-subjonctif",
    minutes=24,
    summary="The subjunctive is the mood of wish, feeling, doubt and necessity: learn the triggers, the present and past forms, and why the imperfect and pluperfect subjunctives are only for reading.",
    objectives=["Form the present subjunctive of regular and common irregular verbs", "Choose subjunctive or indicative after a verb or conjunction, and use the infinitive when the subject does not change",
                "Use the past subjunctive for an action that happened before the main verb", "Recognise the imperfect and pluperfect subjunctive in literary texts"],
    key_concepts=["Subjonctif présent", "Subjonctif passé", "Déclencheurs (volonté, sentiment, doute, nécessité)", "Conjonctions + subjonctif", "Subjonctif imparfait et plus-que-parfait (littéraire)"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("At O Level you met the present subjunctive after *il faut que*. At A Level the subjunctive is a major test of accuracy in Paper 1 (grammar items) and in the essay and translation of Paper 3. A single wrong mood in an essay shows the examiner at once that the rule is not secure."),
        T("The idea is simple. The **indicative** presents something as a fact: *Il vient.* The **subjunctive** presents it as something wished, felt, doubted or needed, not simply stated: *Je veux qu'il vienne.*"),
        H("Forming the present subjunctive"),
        D("The **subjonctif présent** of most verbs is built from the *ils* form of the present indicative, with the stem of that form (remove *-ent*) and the endings **-e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent**. Examples: *ils parlent* gives *que je parle*; *ils finissent* gives *que nous finissions*; *ils vendent* gives *qu'ils vendent*.", title="Definition"),
        T("Verbs with two stems in the present keep both. The *nous* and *vous* forms follow the *nous* stem of the present: *prendre* gives *que je prenne* but *que nous prenions*; *venir* gives *que tu viennes* but *que vous veniez*; *voir* gives *que je voie* but *que nous voyions*; *boire* gives *que je boive* but *que nous buvions*."),
        KEY("Seven irregular verbs to know by heart", "These do not follow the *ils* rule. Learn all six persons of each.", cards=[
            ["être", "que je sois, que tu sois, qu'il soit, que nous soyons, que vous soyez, qu'ils soient"],
            ["avoir", "que j'aie, que tu aies, qu'il ait, que nous ayons, que vous ayez, qu'ils aient"],
            ["aller", "que j'aille, que tu ailles, qu'il aille, que nous allions, que vous alliez, qu'ils aillent"],
            ["faire", "que je fasse, que tu fasses, qu'il fasse, que nous fassions, que vous fassiez, qu'ils fassent"],
            ["pouvoir", "que je puisse, que tu puisses, qu'il puisse, que nous puissions, que vous puissiez, qu'ils puissent"],
            ["savoir", "que je sache, que tu saches, qu'il sache, que nous sachions, que vous sachiez, qu'ils sachent"],
            ["vouloir", "que je veuille, que tu veuilles, qu'il veuille, que nous voulions, que vous vouliez, qu'ils veuillent"]]),
        T("Two more worth noting: *falloir* has *qu'il faille*, and *valoir* has *qu'il vaille* (as in *il vaut mieux que*, which is followed by the subjunctive: *il vaut mieux que tu partes*)."),
        H("When to use it"),
        T("Use the subjunctive in a subordinate clause introduced by *que* after verbs and expressions of four kinds. (1) **Will, wish, order, permission**: *vouloir que, souhaiter que, exiger que, ordonner que, permettre que, préférer que*. (2) **Feeling**: *être content que, regretter que, craindre que, avoir peur que, il est dommage que, s'étonner que*. (3) **Doubt or possibility**: *douter que, il est possible que, il se peut que, il est peu probable que*. (4) **Necessity or judgement**: *il faut que, il est nécessaire que, il est important que, il est normal que, il vaut mieux que*."),
        T("Certain conjunctions also take the subjunctive: *pour que, afin que* (purpose); *bien que, quoique* (concession); *avant que, jusqu'à ce que, en attendant que* (time); *à condition que, pourvu que, à moins que* (condition); *sans que* (absence). The subjunctive also follows a superlative or *le seul, l'unique, le premier, le dernier* when the speaker is giving an opinion: *C'est le meilleur film que j'aie vu.*"),
        T("Verbs of thinking and saying (*penser, croire, trouver, espérer*) take the indicative when affirmative (*Je pense qu'il vient*), and usually the subjunctive when they are negative or questioned in a doubtful sense (*Je ne pense pas qu'il vienne*). *Espérer* is the common exception: *J'espère qu'il viendra.* Another trap: *après que* takes the indicative in careful French (*Après qu'il est parti*), although the subjunctive is often heard."),
        EX("Worked example 1: indicative or subjunctive?", intro="Complete each sentence with the correct form of the verb in brackets.", steps=[
            S("*Il faut que nous (finir) ce travail ce soir.*", text="*Il faut que* is necessity. *Finir*: *ils finissent*, so **finissions**."),
            S("*Je sais qu'il (être) malade.*", text="*Savoir que* states a fact, so the indicative: **est**."),
            S("*Bien qu'elle (avoir) peu de temps, elle nous a aidés.*", text="*Bien que* needs the subjunctive. *Avoir* is irregular: **ait**."),
            S("*Il est certain qu'elle (venir) demain.*", text="*Il est certain que* expresses certainty, so the indicative future: **viendra**."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: subjunctive or infinitive?", intro="Make one sentence from the two parts: *Marie veut* + *Marie réussit* and then *Marie veut* + *Paul réussit*.", steps=[
            S("Same subject (Marie and Marie)", text="Do not use *que*. Use the infinitive: **Marie veut réussir.**"),
            S("Different subjects (Marie and Paul)", text="Use *que* and the subjunctive: **Marie veut que Paul réussisse.**"),
            S("Same rule for conjunctions", text="*Je travaille pour réussir* (same subject) but *Je travaille pour que mes enfants réussissent* (different subjects). With *avant de* and *sans* we also use the infinitive when the subject is the same: *Il est parti sans dire au revoir.*"),
        ]),
        TIP("The test of the infinitive: if the subject of both verbs is the same person, prefer *vouloir + infinitive*, *pour + infinitive*, *avant de + infinitive*, *sans + infinitive*. The subjunctive appears only when the subject changes."),
        H("The past subjunctive"),
        D("The **subjonctif passé** is the present subjunctive of *avoir* or *être* plus the past participle: *que j'aie fini, qu'elle soit partie, qu'ils aient compris*. Use it when the action of the subordinate clause happened **before** the action of the main verb.", title="Definition"),
        T("Compare *Je suis content qu'il vienne* (he comes now or later) with *Je suis content qu'il soit venu* (he has already come). Also *Bien qu'il ait plu toute la nuit, la route est praticable.* The participle agrees as in the passé composé: *Je suis ravie que tes sœurs soient arrivées.*"),
        IMG("alfrl1-subjonctif-choix.svg", alt="Flowchart: does the clause express wish, feeling, doubt or necessity? If the subject is the same, use the infinitive; if it changes, use the subjunctive; for facts use the indicative.", caption="Figure 1. Choosing between subjunctive, indicative and infinitive."),
        H("The imperfect and pluperfect subjunctive"),
        T("In modern writing the **subjonctif imparfait** and **plus-que-parfait** (*que je parlasse, qu'il fût venu*) are found only in literary and very formal texts. You must **recognise** them when you read, but you should not use them in your own essay: the present and past subjunctive replace them, and examiners accept this."),
        KEY("Recognising literary forms", "These appear in classical and nineteenth-century texts that may be set in Paper 2.", cards=[
            ["Imparfait", "*qu'il parlât, qu'elle finît, qu'il fût, qu'elle eût* (third person singular ends in a circumflex *-ât / -ît / -ût*)"],
            ["Plus-que-parfait", "*qu'il eût parlé, qu'elle fût partie* (imparfait of *avoir* or *être* + participle)"],
            ["Meaning", "Translate them as an ordinary English subjunctive or conditional: *Il fallait qu'il parlât* means *he had to speak.*"]]),
        MISTAKE("Using the indicative after *bien que*", "Write *Bien qu'il est fatigué* is wrong. The correct form is *Bien qu'il soit fatigué.* The same applies to *quoique, pour que, avant que, jusqu'à ce que, sans que*. Learn the list of conjunctions as a block."),
        MISTAKE("Using *que* + subjunctive when the subject does not change", "*Je veux que je parte* is wrong. Write *Je veux partir.* Likewise *Elle regrette qu'elle soit partie* should be *Elle regrette d'être partie.*"),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the stems", "Do not write *que nous prennions* or *que vous venions*. The *nous* and *vous* forms use the *nous* stem: *que nous prenions, que vous veniez.*"),
        QUIZ("Which sentence is correct?", ["Je ne crois pas qu'il vient.", "Je ne crois pas qu'il vienne.", "Je crois qu'il vienne.", "Je crois qu'il venait demain."], 1,
             "*Je ne crois pas que* expresses doubt, so the subjunctive: *vienne*. The affirmative *Je crois que* takes the indicative (*vient* or *viendra*).",
             hint="Is the main clause affirmative or negative?",
             feedback=["The negative *ne pas croire que* usually needs the subjunctive.", "", "The affirmative *croire que* takes the indicative, not the subjunctive.", "*Venait* (imperfect) with *demain* does not fit; the tenses do not agree."]),
        QUIZ("Choose the correct form: *Il est dommage que vous ... hier soir.*", ["n'êtes pas venus", "ne soyez pas venus", "ne veniez pas", "ne venez pas"], 1,
             "*Il est dommage que* is a feeling, so the subjunctive. The action (*hier soir*) came before the feeling, so the past subjunctive: *ne soyez pas venus*.",
             hint="Look at *hier soir*: has the action already happened?",
             feedback=["The indicative is wrong after *il est dommage que*.", "", "*Veniez* is a present-time form (present subjunctive or imperfect): it does not show an action already finished.", "*Venez* is the indicative present: wrong mood and wrong time."]),
        PQ("Put the verbs in brackets in the correct form. (a) Il faut que tu (être) à l'heure. (b) Je suis heureux que vous (réussir) l'examen la semaine dernière. (c) Nous resterons ici jusqu'à ce qu'il (pouvoir) partir.",
           "(a) **sois**: *être* is irregular. (b) **ayez réussi**: feeling, and the success came before the happiness (past subjunctive). (c) **puisse**: *jusqu'à ce que* takes the subjunctive; *pouvoir* gives *puisse*."),
        PQ("Rewrite correctly if necessary: (a) *Je veux que je réussisse.* (b) *Bien qu'il pleut, nous sortons.* (c) *Il est probable qu'il viendra.*",
           "(a) **Je veux réussir.** The subject is the same, so the infinitive is used. (b) **Bien qu'il pleuve, nous sortons.** *Bien que* needs the subjunctive. (c) Correct: *il est probable que* expresses a likelihood, not doubt, and takes the indicative (*il est possible que* would take the subjunctive: *qu'il vienne*). Check your textbook for the list of impersonal expressions."),
        PQ("Write three sentences about your school, each using a different trigger: one of will, one of feeling and one conjunction of concession.",
           "Model: *Le proviseur veut que les élèves portent l'uniforme.* (will) / *Je suis content que nous ayons un nouveau laboratoire.* (feeling) / *Bien que le trajet soit long, j'arrive toujours à l'heure.* (concession). Check the mood after each trigger and that each *que* clause has its own subject."),
        TIP("In Paper 1, an item on the subjunctive usually gives four forms of the same verb. First decide the mood (trigger), then the time (present or past), then check the person ending."),
        SUM("The present subjunctive uses the *ils* stem plus -e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent; learn the irregulars être, avoir, aller, faire, pouvoir, savoir, vouloir.",
            "It follows will, feeling, doubt and necessity, and conjunctions such as *bien que, pour que, avant que, jusqu'à ce que*.",
            "If the subject does not change, use the infinitive instead of *que* + subjunctive.",
            "Use the past subjunctive for an action earlier than the main verb.",
            "The imperfect and pluperfect subjunctive are for recognition in literary texts only."),
    ],
))

# ================================================================== 2. Le conditionnel
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Le conditionnel",
    title="Le conditionnel : hypothèse, politesse et nuances",
    slug="frl1-conditionnel",
    minutes=24,
    summary="The conditional expresses what would happen, a polite request, an unconfirmed report, regret or the future in the past; learn the three si-sentence patterns and the second form of the past conditional.",
    objectives=["Form the present and past conditional", "Build the three types of si-sentence without ever writing si + conditional", "Use the conditional for politeness, reported information, advice, regret and the future in the past", "Recognise the literary second form of the past conditional"],
    key_concepts=["Conditionnel présent et passé", "Phrases hypothétiques (si)", "Conditionnel de politesse", "Conditionnel d'information non confirmée", "Futur dans le passé"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("The conditional is more than the 'would' tense. In a newspaper, *Le ministre serait en visite à Garoua* means the journalist is **not sure**. In a shop, *Je voudrais deux kilos de tomates* is polite. In an essay, *Si les routes étaient meilleures, les marchés seraient mieux approvisionnés* builds an argument. Each use is tested at A Level."),
        H("Forming the conditional"),
        D("The **conditionnel présent** is the **future stem** plus the **imperfect endings** -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient: *je parlerais, tu finirais, il vendrait, nous irions, vous seriez, elles auraient*. The **conditionnel passé** is the present conditional of *avoir* or *être* plus the past participle: *j'aurais parlé, elle serait venue*.", title="Definition"),
        T("Irregular future stems are the same in the conditional: *ser-* (être), *aur-* (avoir), *ir-* (aller), *fer-* (faire), *pourr-* (pouvoir), *saur-* (savoir), *voudr-* (vouloir), *viendr-* (venir), *verr-* (voir), *devr-* (devoir), *faudr-* (falloir), *enverr-* (envoyer), *courr-* (courir). Be careful: *pourrais* has two r, and *je verrais* differs from *je voyais*."),
        H("The three si-sentences"),
        KEY("Si + tense, result + tense", "Memorise the pairs. The *si*-clause never contains a conditional.", cards=[
            ["Real or likely (open condition)", "*Si* + présent, **futur** (or présent or impératif): *Si tu étudies, tu réussiras.*"],
            ["Unlikely or imaginary now", "*Si* + **imparfait**, **conditionnel présent**: *Si j'avais une voiture, je t'accompagnerais.*"],
            ["Impossible, in the past", "*Si* + **plus-que-parfait**, **conditionnel passé**: *Si tu avais étudié, tu aurais réussi.*"],
            ["Mixed", "*Si* + plus-que-parfait, conditionnel présent: *Si j'avais accepté ce poste, je vivrais à Yaoundé aujourd'hui.*"]]),
        IMG("alfrl1-conditionnel-si.svg", alt="Three-row table pairing the tense of the si-clause with the tense of the main clause for open, unlikely and impossible conditions.", caption="Figure 1. The three si-sentences."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the three types", intro="Complete: *Si nous (avoir) plus de temps, nous (visiter) le marché de Mokolo.* Then change it to the past (impossible).", steps=[
            S("Decide the type", text="The speaker does not have the time now: imaginary, so *si* + imparfait and conditionnel présent."),
            S("Complete", text="**Si nous avions plus de temps, nous visiterions le marché de Mokolo.**"),
            S("Change to the past", text="Plus-que-parfait and conditionnel passé: **Si nous avions eu plus de temps, nous aurions visité le marché de Mokolo.**"),
        ], check=r"\text{avions (imparfait)} \to \text{visiterions};\ \text{avions eu (plus-que-parfait)} \to \text{aurions visité}"),
        T("A note on *si*: it takes the conditional only in an **indirect question** (*Je me demande si elle accepterait*), where it means *whether*, not *if*. That is why *Si j'aurais su* is a classic error: write *Si j'avais su, je serais venu.*"),
        H("Other uses of the conditional"),
        KEY("Six more jobs for the conditional", "Each corresponds to a different shade of meaning.", cards=[
            ["Politeness", "*Pourriez-vous m'aider ? Je voudrais un renseignement.*"],
            ["Advice, reproach, regret", "*Tu devrais te reposer.* *Tu aurais dû m'appeler.* (past conditional of *devoir*)"],
            ["Unconfirmed information", "*Selon la presse, deux cents personnes auraient été évacuées.* The writer does not guarantee the fact."],
            ["Future in the past", "*Il a annoncé qu'il viendrait.* (He said: *Je viendrai.*)"],
            ["Imagination and wish", "*On dirait qu'il va pleuvoir. J'aimerais tant voyager.*"],
            ["Concession, with *même si* and *au cas où*", "*Au cas où il pleuvrait, nous resterions ici.* Note that *même si* follows the *si* rules: *même s'il pleuvait*."]]),
        EX("Worked example 2: reported speech and unconfirmed news", intro="Put the following into French reported speech: *Le directeur dit : « Je ferai le point demain. »* (past reporting verb) and rewrite *On dit que le président est à Douala* as a newspaper hedge.", steps=[
            S("Reported speech in the past", text="Future becomes future in the past, and *demain* becomes *le lendemain*: **Le directeur a dit qu'il ferait le point le lendemain.**"),
            S("The hedge", text="Use the conditional to signal that the news is unconfirmed: **Le président serait à Douala.**"),
            S("Check", text="*Ferait* is the conditional of *faire* (*fer-* + *-ait*). *Serait* is *ser-* + *-ait*."),
        ]),
        H("The second form of the past conditional"),
        T("In literary texts the past conditional has a second form: the pluperfect subjunctive of *avoir* or *être*. *Il eût fallu* means *il aurait fallu*, and *Il eût mieux valu se taire* means *il aurait mieux valu se taire*. You need only recognise it. (Check your textbook for how much your school expects.)"),
        MISTAKE("*Si* + conditional", "Never write *Si tu viendrais, je serais content.* Write *Si tu venais, je serais content.* The conditional belongs in the result clause only."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the future and the conditional after a past verb", "*Il a dit qu'il viendra* is wrong. After a verb in the past use the conditional: *Il a dit qu'il viendrait.* The same pattern works for *pensait que, savait que, promettait que*."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the *-r-* of irregular stems", "*Je pouvais* and *je pourrais* are different tenses. *Je vois* and *je verrais*, *j'envoie* and *j'enverrais*, *il faut* and *il faudrait* are also easy to confuse."),
        QUIZ("Choose the correct sentence.", ["Si j'aurais le temps, je viendrais.", "Si j'avais le temps, je viendrais.", "Si j'ai le temps, je viendrais.", "Si j'avais eu le temps, je viendrais hier."], 1,
             "The imaginary condition needs *si* + imparfait and conditionnel présent: *Si j'avais le temps, je viendrais.*",
             hint="What tense must follow *si* in an unlikely condition?",
             feedback=["*Si* is never followed by the conditional.", "", "*Si* + présent needs a future (or présent), not a conditional.", "With plus-que-parfait the result must be the past conditional: *je serais venu*."]),
        QUIZ("Which sentence shows that the journalist is not certain of the news?", ["Le ministre arrive à Bertoua.", "Le ministre arriverait à Bertoua.", "Le ministre est arrivé à Bertoua.", "Le ministre arrivera à Bertoua."], 1,
             "The conditional (*arriverait*) is the French way of reporting something unconfirmed.",
             hint="Which tense is used for rumour?",
             feedback=["The present states a fact.", "", "The passé composé states a fact in the past.", "The future states a prediction, not doubt."]),
        PQ("Put the verbs in the correct tense. (a) Si elle (savoir) la réponse, elle la donnerait. (b) Si vous (avoir) faim, il y a du riz. (c) Si nous (partir) plus tôt hier, nous (arriver) à l'heure.",
           "(a) **savait** (imparfait, with *donnerait*). (b) **avez** (présent, result in the present). (c) **étions partis ... serions arrivés**: *hier* shows the past, so plus-que-parfait and conditionnel passé (*nous serions arrivés*, with the participle agreeing with *nous*)."),
        PQ("Turn into polite requests: (a) *Je veux un verre d'eau.* (b) *Peux-tu fermer la porte ?* (c) *Vous devez vous reposer.* (advice)",
           "(a) **Je voudrais un verre d'eau.** (b) **Pourrais-tu fermer la porte ?** (c) **Vous devriez vous reposer.**"),
        PQ("Write a sentence of regret using the past conditional of *devoir* and a sentence reporting a rumour with the conditional.",
           "Model: *J'aurais dû réviser davantage.* / *Le pont serait fermé à cause des pluies.* The first uses *aurais dû* (past conditional); the second uses *serait* to show the news is unconfirmed."),
        TIP("In Paper 1 and in your essays, circle the *si*. Check the tense of the *si*-clause first: it tells you the type, and the type tells you the tense of the main clause. In translation, English *would* may be a conditional, an imperfect of habit (*he would visit every Sunday* = *il rendait visite chaque dimanche*) or a past modal; see the translation lessons."),
        SUM("Conditional = future stem + imperfect endings; past conditional = conditional of *avoir* or *être* + participle.",
            "Never use the conditional in the *si*-clause: *si* + présent, futur; *si* + imparfait, conditionnel; *si* + plus-que-parfait, conditionnel passé.",
            "Other uses: politeness, advice and regret, unconfirmed news, the future in the past, wish and imagination.",
            "The second form (*il eût fallu*) is literary: recognise it."),
    ],
))

# ================================================================== 3. Les pronoms relatifs composés
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Les pronoms relatifs composés",
    title="Les pronoms relatifs composés : lequel, auquel, duquel et dont",
    slug="frl1-pronoms-relatifs-composes",
    minutes=22,
    summary="After a preposition a relative clause needs lequel and its forms (or qui for people): learn the contractions auquel and duquel, when to use dont instead, and how to link two sentences into one.",
    objectives=["Form and agree lequel, laquelle, lesquels, lesquelles with their contractions", "Choose between qui, lequel, dont and où after a preposition", "Join two sentences into one complex sentence with the right relative pronoun", "Handle ce dont, ce à quoi and relative clauses placed after a long prepositional phrase"],
    key_concepts=["Préposition + lequel", "Contractions auquel / duquel", "Dont", "Où", "Ce dont, ce à quoi"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("English lets a preposition stay at the end of a clause: *the pen I write with*. French cannot. The preposition must come **before** the relative pronoun: *le stylo avec lequel j'écris*. This is why the compound relative pronouns are essential for good written French and for translation."),
        T("In Paper 1 you will be asked to choose the correct pronoun in a sentence. In Paper 3 you need them to turn short sentences into the longer, more elegant sentences that an A Level essay demands."),
        H("The forms"),
        D("The **compound relative pronouns** are *lequel, laquelle, lesquels, lesquelles*. They agree in gender and number with the noun they replace (the antecedent) and are used after a preposition, especially when the antecedent is a thing.", title="Definition"),
        KEY("The table of forms", "With *à* and *de* the masculine singular and both plurals contract (*laquelle* does not).", cards=[
            ["After most prepositions", "lequel, laquelle, lesquels, lesquelles: *avec lequel, pour laquelle, par lesquels, sans lesquelles*"],
            ["With à", "auquel, à laquelle, auxquels, auxquelles: *le projet auquel il pense*"],
            ["With de (and à côté de, près de, au bord de ...)", "duquel, de laquelle, desquels, desquelles: *la rue au bout de laquelle ...*"]]),
        T("Examples: *La raison pour laquelle il a refusé reste inconnue.* *Les routes par lesquelles nous sommes passés étaient boueuses.* *Le problème auquel nous pensons est sérieux.* *Les questions auxquelles elle a répondu étaient difficiles.*"),
        H("People: qui or lequel?"),
        T("After a preposition and for **people**, use *qui*: *l'homme avec qui je travaille, la femme à qui j'ai écrit*. *Lequel* is also correct for people but sounds more formal. For **things**, use *lequel*: *la société pour laquelle elle travaille*. After *entre, parmi* and *d'entre*, even for people, *lequel* is required: *les candidats parmi lesquels se trouvait ma sœur*."),
        T("Note that *qui* is invariable and cannot be used with a thing after a preposition: *l'entreprise pour qui elle travaille* is wrong in careful French."),
        H("Dont: the pronoun for de"),
        D("**Dont** replaces *de* plus a noun and means *of whom, of which, whose*. It cannot be preceded by a preposition. It is used (a) with verbs that take *de*: *le film dont tout le monde parle* (parler de), *les résultats dont nous sommes fiers* (être fier de); (b) for possession: *l'élève dont le frère est médecin*; (c) with a noun complement: *le village dont j'ai vu l'église*.", title="Definition"),
        T("Word order after *dont*: the noun it qualifies follows with its article and no possessive adjective. We say *la dame dont le fils est médecin*, not *dont son fils*."),
        T("**Dont or duquel?** If the noun that *de* depends on is **not** preceded by a preposition, use *dont*: *Le roman dont j'ai lu la fin.* If it follows a preposition, use *duquel, de laquelle ...*: *Le roman à la fin duquel on découvre le coupable.* Lengthy prepositions ending in *de* (*à côté de, au bord de, au cours de, à l'aide de, au milieu de, près de*) also take the compound form: *le lac au bord duquel nous avons campé, les années au cours desquelles il a voyagé.*"),
        H("Où and ce dont"),
        T("*Où* replaces a place or a time: *la ville où je suis né, le jour où il est arrivé, d'où, par où, jusqu'où*. When the antecedent is a whole idea or has no noun, use *ce qui* (subject), *ce que* (object), *ce dont* (de), *ce à quoi* (à): *Voilà ce dont j'ai besoin. Dites-moi ce à quoi vous pensez.* After a preposition with no antecedent, *quoi* is used: *Il a signé, après quoi il est sorti.*"),
        EX("Worked example 1: joining two sentences", intro="Join: *Voici le stylo. J'écris avec ce stylo.*", steps=[
            S("Find the repeated noun", text="*Ce stylo* in the second sentence repeats *le stylo*."),
            S("Choose the preposition", text="*Écrire avec* needs *avec*, and a thing is the antecedent, so *avec lequel* (masculine singular)."),
            S("Join", text="**Voici le stylo avec lequel j'écris.**"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: dont or compound?", intro="Join: (a) *Les élèves ont réussi. Le professeur parle de ces élèves.* (b) *La rivière traverse la ville. Nous avons campé au bord de cette rivière.*", steps=[
            S("(a) *Parler de* gives *de + ces élèves*", text="No preposition stands before the noun, so **dont**: *Les élèves dont le professeur parle ont réussi.*"),
            S("(b) *Au bord de cette rivière*", text="The preposition *au bord de* is before the noun, so the compound form: *de laquelle* (feminine) joins with *au bord*: **La rivière au bord de laquelle nous avons campé traverse la ville.**"),
            S("Check", text="In (b) *rivière* is feminine singular, so *laquelle*. In *au bord duquel* we would need a masculine noun, such as *le lac*."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: parmi and dont with a noun", intro="Join: *Nous avons interrogé dix élèves. Parmi ces élèves, trois sont redoublants.* Then: *Nous parlons d'un livre. L'auteur de ce livre est camerounais.*", steps=[
            S("*Parmi* + people", text="*Parmi* requires *lequel*, even for persons: **Nous avons interrogé dix élèves, parmi lesquels trois sont redoublants.**"),
            S("The second pair", text="*L'auteur de ce livre* is *de* + noun, with no preposition before *l'auteur*, so **dont**: **Nous parlons d'un livre dont l'auteur est camerounais.**"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using *que* or *qui* after a preposition for a thing", "Wrong: *Le sujet de que nous parlons.* Right: *Le sujet dont nous parlons.* Wrong: *La raison pour qui il est parti.* Right: *La raison pour laquelle il est parti.*"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the contraction", "Wrong: *Le projet à lequel nous pensons.* Right: *Le projet auquel nous pensons.* Wrong: *Les arbres près de lesquels ...* Right: *Les arbres près desquels ...*"),
        MISTAKE("Using a possessive after *dont*", "Wrong: *L'homme dont sa fille est médecin.* Right: *L'homme dont la fille est médecin.*"),
        TIP("English often drops the relative pronoun: *the book I read*. French never does: *le livre que j'ai lu*. When translating, always supply the pronoun and move any preposition to the front: *the town I come from* = *la ville d'où je viens*."),
        QUIZ("Choose the correct pronoun: *La salle dans ... nous avons passé l'examen est grande.*", ["laquelle", "dont", "qui", "lequel"], 0,
             "*Salle* is feminine singular and follows *dans*: *dans laquelle*.",
             hint="What is the gender of *salle*, and what comes before the blank?",
             feedback=["", "*Dont* cannot follow *dans*; it replaces *de* + noun.", "*Qui* after a preposition is used only for persons.", "*Lequel* is masculine; *salle* is feminine."]),
        QUIZ("Choose the correct pronoun: *Voici le village ... j'ai visité l'école.*", ["auquel", "duquel", "dont", "où"], 2,
             "*J'ai visité l'école de ce village* contains *de + noun* with no preposition before *l'école*, so *dont*.",
             hint="Is there a preposition before *l'école* in the original sentence?",
             feedback=["*Auquel* replaces *à* + noun, not *de*.", "*Duquel* is used only after a preposition ending in *de*, such as *à côté de*.", "", "*Où* would mean *in which*; the sentence has the school belonging to the village."]),
        PQ("Join with the correct pronoun. (a) *La ville est célèbre. Je suis originaire de cette ville.* (b) *Les amis sont partis. Je comptais sur ces amis.* (c) *La maison est ancienne. Nous habitons près de cette maison.*",
           "(a) **La ville dont je suis originaire est célèbre.** (b) **Les amis sur lesquels je comptais sont partis.** (*Compter sur* with people could also give *sur qui*.) (c) **La maison près de laquelle nous habitons est ancienne.**"),
        PQ("Complete with *ce qui, ce que, ce dont* or *ce à quoi*. (a) Je ne comprends pas ... vous voulez. (b) Dites-moi ... vous avez besoin. (c) ... compte, c'est l'effort.",
           "(a) **ce que** (object of *voulez*). (b) **ce dont** (*avoir besoin de*). (c) **Ce qui** (subject of *compte*)."),
        PQ("Translate into French: *The road along which we travelled was long.* and *The decision I disagree with has been cancelled.*",
           "**La route le long de laquelle nous avons voyagé était longue.** (*le long de* ends in *de*: *de laquelle*.) **La décision avec laquelle je ne suis pas d'accord a été annulée.** (*Être d'accord avec* requires *avec*.)"),
        SUM("After a preposition, use *lequel* (things), *qui* (people) and its contractions *auquel, duquel* and plurals.",
            "*Dont* replaces *de* + noun and is used when no preposition precedes the noun it refers to; use *duquel* after a preposition ending in *de*.",
            "*Où* replaces place and time; *ce dont* and *ce à quoi* are used when there is no noun antecedent.",
            "Never leave a preposition at the end of the clause, and never omit the relative pronoun."),
    ],
))

# ================================================================== 4. La nominalisation
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="La nominalisation",
    title="La nominalisation : transformer les verbes et les adjectifs en noms",
    slug="frl1-nominalisation",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Nominalisation turns verbs and adjectives into nouns (arriver, l'arrivée; rapide, la rapidité) to write concise, formal French in essays, summaries and news headlines; learn the suffix families and the order of the new noun phrase.",
    objectives=["Turn a verbal clause into a noun phrase and keep the agent and the object clear", "Recognise the main suffix families (-tion, -ment, -age, -ance, -ité, -eur) and the common irregular pairs", "Use nominalisation for concision in essays, headlines and summaries without making sentences heavy", "Know when to keep the verb"],
    key_concepts=["Nominalisation", "Style nominal", "Suffixes de noms", "Complément du nom (de + agent / objet)", "Équivalents de subordonnées (dès, après, en raison de)"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Compare *Les prix ont augmenté, ce qui a inquiété les ménages* with *L'augmentation des prix a inquiété les ménages*. The second sentence is shorter and more formal: the verb *augmenter* has become the noun *l'augmentation*. French prefers this abstract style far more than English does, especially in newspapers, official documents, reports and dissertations. It is a key skill for a polished Paper 3 essay."),
        H("What is nominalisation?"),
        D("**La nominalisation** is the process of turning a verb, an adjective or a whole clause into a noun or a noun phrase: *arriver > l'arrivée*, *rapide > la rapidité*, *Quand le ministre est arrivé > À l'arrivée du ministre*.", title="Definition"),
        T("The result is the **style nominal**. It is used in newspaper headlines (*Hausse du prix du carburant*), in administrative French, in summaries and in analytical essays, because nouns allow dense information in a short space."),
        H("Building the new noun phrase"),
        T("Follow three steps. (1) Find the verb and its noun (see the table below). (2) Put the **subject** of the old verb after the noun with *de*: *le ministre est arrivé* gives *l'arrivée du ministre*. (3) If there was also an **object**, the object takes *de* and the agent is introduced by *par*: *la police a arrêté le suspect* gives *l'arrestation du suspect par la police*."),
        KEY("Common verb-noun pairs", "Learn the nouns with their gender.", cards=[
            ["-tion, -sion (feminine)", "augmenter > l'augmentation, décider > la décision, construire > la construction, réduire > la réduction, adapter > l'adaptation, disparaître > la disparition"],
            ["-ement (masculine)", "lancer > le lancement, élargir > l'élargissement, développer > le développement"],
            ["-age (masculine)", "apprendre > l'apprentissage, nettoyer > le nettoyage, atterrir > l'atterrissage"],
            ["-ance, -ence (feminine)", "naître > la naissance, croître > la croissance, souffrir > la souffrance"],
            ["Nouns from the participle or the stem", "arriver > l'arrivée, découvrir > la découverte, réussir > la réussite, rentrer > la rentrée, vendre > la vente"],
            ["Irregular or unrelated", "partir > le départ, revenir > le retour, choisir > le choix, échouer > l'échec"]]),
        KEY("Adjective to noun", "Many adjectives give a noun in *-té, -esse, -eur, -ance*.", cards=[
            ["-ité", "rapide > la rapidité, généreux > la générosité, vrai > la vérité"],
            ["-té / -eté", "difficile > la difficulté, pauvre > la pauvreté, sûr > la sûreté (or la sécurité)"],
            ["-eur, -esse", "long > la longueur, lent > la lenteur, faible > la faiblesse"],
            ["Others", "important > l'importance, fort > la force. Check a dictionary when unsure."]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: clauses to noun phrases", intro="Rewrite using a noun: (a) *Les élèves sont arrivés en retard, ce qui a surpris le directeur.* (b) *Parce que les prix ont augmenté, les ménages ont réduit leurs achats.*", steps=[
            S("(a) Choose the noun", text="*Arriver* gives *l'arrivée*, with the agent *des élèves*: *L'arrivée tardive des élèves a surpris le directeur.* The adverb *en retard* becomes the adjective *tardive*."),
            S("(b) Choose a link", text="*Parce que* becomes *en raison de* or *à cause de*, followed by the noun *l'augmentation*: **En raison de l'augmentation des prix, les ménages ont réduit leurs achats.**"),
            S("Check", text="Each time the verb becomes a noun; the sentence is shorter, and the adverbs become adjectives."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: object and agent", intro="Nominalise: *Les rebelles ont détruit le pont.* and *Les ingénieurs ont construit le barrage en deux ans.*", steps=[
            S("First sentence", text="*Détruire* gives *la destruction*. Object: *du pont*. Agent: *par les rebelles*. **La destruction du pont par les rebelles.**"),
            S("Second sentence", text="*Construire* gives *la construction*. Object: *du barrage*. Agent: *par les ingénieurs*. **La construction du barrage par les ingénieurs en deux ans.**"),
            S("Headline style", text="A headline drops the article: **Destruction du pont par des rebelles.**"),
        ]),
        T("**Temporal and logical connectors become prepositions.** *Quand le ministre est arrivé* gives *À l'arrivée du ministre* (or *Dès l'arrivée*). *Après qu'ils ont terminé les travaux* gives *Après la fin des travaux*. *Parce que* gives *à cause de, en raison de, grâce à*. *Bien que les routes soient mauvaises* gives *Malgré le mauvais état des routes*."),
        H("Style: when to use it, when to keep the verb"),
        T("Nominalisation brings concision, formality and abstraction. It is ideal in a dissertation introduction (*L'essor des technologies numériques soulève la question de l'avenir du livre*) and in a summary. But too many nouns make a heavy, impersonal text, with sequences of *de de de*. If a sentence becomes hard to read, keep the verb. Also avoid weak verbs such as *faire* and *effectuer* with a noun when a strong verb will do: *effectuer une réduction* is heavier than *réduire*."),
        IMG("alfrl1-nominalisation.svg", alt="Three-row diagram turning a verbal clause into a noun phrase: verb to noun, subject to de plus agent, conjunction to preposition.", caption="Figure 1. From clause to noun phrase."),
        MISTAKE("Wrong gender or suffix", "Do not invent nouns by adding *-tion* to every verb. We say *le lancement* (not *la lancation*), *l'arrivée* (not *l'arrivation*), *le choix* (not *la choisission*). Learn the pairs as vocabulary."),
        MISTAKE("Losing the agent or the object", "*La destruction du pont* does not say who destroyed it. If the agent matters, add *par les rebelles*. Also be careful with *de*: *la peur du soldat* can mean the soldier is afraid or is feared. Rephrase when the meaning is unclear."),
        MISTAKE("Keeping the adverb", "Adverbs become adjectives. *Il a réagi rapidement* gives *sa réaction rapide* (not *sa réaction rapidement*)."),
        QUIZ("Which noun phrase corresponds to *Le gouvernement a décidé de construire une route* ?", ["La décision du gouvernement de construire une route", "Le gouvernement de décider de construire une route", "La décider du gouvernement", "La décision du gouvernement construisant une route"], 0,
             "*Décider* gives *la décision*; the agent follows with *de*, and the infinitive complement is kept with *de*.",
             hint="Find the noun linked to *décider*, then put the agent after it.",
             feedback=["", "The verb is not turned into a noun.", "*Décider* is the verb, not the noun.", "*Construisant* (present participle) is not a normal complement of *décision*; use *de + infinitive*."]),
        QUIZ("Choose the correct noun for *rapide*:", ["la rapidation", "la rapidité", "la rapideur", "le rapidement"], 1,
             "Adjectives in *-ide* usually give a noun in *-idité*: *rapide > la rapidité*.",
             hint="Think of *humide > l'humidité*.",
             feedback=["No such word.", "", "No such word.", "*Rapidement* is an adverb, not a noun."]),
        PQ("Nominalise the underlined idea: *Dès que le président est arrivé, la cérémonie a commencé.* (Start with *Dès*.)",
           "**Dès l'arrivée du président, la cérémonie a commencé.** *Arriver* gives *l'arrivée*; the subject *le président* follows with *de*; the subordinate clause becomes a prepositional phrase."),
        PQ("Rewrite with a noun: *Comme la population croît rapidement, les services publics manquent de moyens.*",
           "**En raison de la croissance rapide de la population, les services publics manquent de moyens.** *Croître* gives *la croissance*; *rapidement* becomes *rapide*, agreeing with *croissance* (feminine)."),
        PQ("Give a newspaper headline for: *Le prix du riz a baissé de dix pour cent.*",
           "**Baisse de dix pour cent du prix du riz.** or **Baisse du prix du riz : moins dix pour cent.** *Baisser* gives *la baisse*; the article is dropped in a headline."),
        TIP("In your Paper 3 essay, aim for a mix. One or two nominalised sentences in the introduction and the conclusion give the impression of mastery; a whole text in the style nominal reads like a report."),
        SUM("Nominalisation turns verbs and adjectives into nouns for concision and formality; learn verb-noun pairs with their gender.",
            "Subject becomes *de* + agent; if there is an object, the object takes *de* and the agent takes *par*.",
            "Connectors become prepositions: *quand > à l'arrivée de*, *parce que > en raison de*, *bien que > malgré*.",
            "Adverbs become adjectives; do not overload the sentence; keep a verb when clarity needs it."),
    ],
))

# ================================================================== 5. L'inversion stylistique
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="L'inversion stylistique",
    title="L'inversion stylistique : placer le sujet après le verbe",
    slug="frl1-inversion-stylistique",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Stylistic inversion puts the subject after the verb after certain adverbs (peut-être, à peine, ainsi), in relative clauses and quotations; learn the rule for pronoun and noun subjects, the hyphen and the -t-, and when it is wise to use it.",
    objectives=["Apply simple and complex inversion with the correct hyphens and -t-", "Use the main adverbs that trigger inversion at the start of a sentence", "Use inversion in relative clauses, after que and in dialogue tags", "Recognise literary inversions and know how often to use them"],
    key_concepts=["Inversion simple", "Inversion complexe", "Adverbes déclencheurs", "Incise", "Inversion dans la relative"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In an ordinary French sentence the subject comes before the verb: *Il viendra.* **Stylistic inversion** reverses the order for effect and formality: *Peut-être viendra-t-il.* It appears in literary texts (Paper 2), in formal essays (Paper 3) and in Paper 1 items, where you may be asked to complete a sentence with the correct inverted form."),
        H("The two kinds of inversion"),
        D("In **simple inversion** a pronoun subject follows the verb and is joined to it with a hyphen: *Peut-être est-il malade.* In **complex inversion** a noun subject stays before the verb and a pronoun that repeats it follows the verb: *Peut-être le ministre viendra-t-il.* If the verb ends in a vowel and the pronoun is *il, elle* or *on*, a **-t-** is inserted between them: *A-t-il compris ? Viendra-t-elle ? Parle-t-on ?*", title="Definition"),
        T("With a noun subject and no complex pronoun, simple inversion with the noun also exists after some adverbs and in relative clauses: *Ainsi naquit la république.* *Dans la cour se dressait un manguier.*"),
        H("Adverbs that trigger inversion"),
        KEY("Common triggers at the start of a sentence", "Inversion is used with these adverbs and expressions in formal French.", cards=[
            ["peut-être, sans doute", "*Peut-être a-t-il oublié. Sans doute avons-nous raison.* (Both can be used without inversion if followed by *que*: *Peut-être qu'il a oublié.*)"],
            ["à peine ... que", "*À peine était-elle arrivée que la pluie tomba.* (Hardly had she arrived when ...)"],
            ["ainsi (thus), aussi (therefore)", "*Ainsi s'acheva la réunion.* *Il était tard ; aussi avons-nous décidé de partir.* (*Aussi* at the head of a clause means *therefore*.)"],
            ["en vain, du moins, tout au plus, encore", "*En vain essaya-t-il de la convaincre. Du moins est-il honnête. Encore faut-il être patient.*"]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: forming the inversion", intro="Rewrite with inversion: (a) *Peut-être elle a raison.* (b) *Sans doute le directeur viendra.* (c) *À peine ils étaient sortis qu'il a plu.*", steps=[
            S("(a) Pronoun subject, verb ends in a vowel?", text="*Elle a* ends in *a* and the subject is *elle*, so insert **-t-**: **Peut-être a-t-elle raison.**"),
            S("(b) Noun subject", text="Complex inversion: **Sans doute le directeur viendra-t-il.** (*Viendra* ends in *a*, so *-t-* with *il*.)"),
            S("(c) *À peine* with a *que* clause", text="**À peine étaient-ils sortis qu'il a plu.** (*Étaient* ends in *t*, no extra *-t-*.) In formal narrative the second verb is often in the passé simple: *qu'il plut*."),
        ]),
        H("Inversion in other positions"),
        T("**After a quotation (the incise):** the verb of speech follows the quotation with inversion: *« Je viendrai, dit-il. » « Pourquoi pars-tu ? demanda Marie. »* (If the subject is a noun, simple inversion: *demanda Marie*; with a pronoun: *dit-il, répondit-elle*.)"),
        T("**In a relative clause or after *que*,** when the subject is a noun: *Voici le livre que lit Marie.* *Les paroles que prononça le président ont marqué les esprits.* *La lettre qu'a écrite le directeur.* (Notice the agreement: the participle *écrite* agrees with the object *que*, which stands for *la lettre*.) Also with *où*: *les villes où vivent les migrants*, and in questions: *Où habite ton cousin ? Quand arrive le train ?* Inversion is more natural here than in spoken French, where we hear *Où est-ce que ton cousin habite ?*"),
        T("**Place or time first in narrative:** *Au fond de la cour se dressait un manguier. Sur la place du marché résonnaient les cris des vendeurs.* The inversion puts the subject, the new information, at the end, a favourite technique in descriptive writing and in Paper 2 prose."),
        T("**Wishes and concessions (recognition):** *Puissiez-vous réussir !* (wish, with the subjunctive), *Fût-il riche, il reste modeste.* (even if he were rich), *Vive la liberté !* These are literary or fixed expressions."),
        EX("Worked example 2: choose the right form", intro="Complete: *Il était tard ; aussi (nous / décider) de rentrer.* and *Les arbres que (planter / mon grand-père) ont grandi.*", steps=[
            S("*Aussi* at the head of the clause", text="*Aussi* means *therefore*, and a pronoun subject follows the verb: **aussi avons-nous décidé de rentrer.**"),
            S("Relative clause with a noun subject", text="The noun subject follows the verb and the *que* object stays in front: **Les arbres que plantait mon grand-père ont grandi.** (imparfait, because the habit is in the past); passé composé would be *qu'a plantés mon grand-père* (agreement with *arbres*)."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the hyphen or the -t-", "Write *A-t-il compris ?*, not *A il compris ?*. The hyphen joins verb and pronoun; the *-t-* is inserted only when the verb ends in a vowel (*parle-t-il*, *viendra-t-elle*), not when it ends in *t* or *d* (*vient-il*, *dit-elle*, *fait-on*, *répond-il*)."),
        MISTAKE("Inverting *je* freely", "With *je*, inversion is rare and limited to a few verbs (*puis-je, suis-je, ai-je, dois-je*). With other verbs use *est-ce que* (*Est-ce que je parle trop vite ?*) instead of an awkward inversion."),
        MISTAKE("Overuse", "Inversion is a spice. Two or three well-placed inversions in an essay show control; one in every sentence sounds artificial."),
        TIP("In English, 'Hardly had she arrived when ...' and 'Never had I seen ...' use the same device. French inversion often translates it, but French also inverts where English would not (after *peut-être, ainsi, aussi, du moins*). Do not copy English word order after these adverbs."),
        QUIZ("Which sentence is correct?", ["Peut-être il viendra demain.", "Peut-être viendra-t-il demain.", "Peut-être viendra il demain.", "Peut-être vient-il-t demain."], 1,
             "After *peut-être* we invert; the verb *viendra* ends in a vowel, so *-t-* is added before *il*.",
             hint="Does the verb end in a vowel? What must be placed before *il*?",
             feedback=["Formal French inverts after *peut-être* (or uses *peut-être que il* in speech).", "", "The hyphens and the *-t-* are missing.", "The *-t-* goes between verb and pronoun, not at the end."]),
        QUIZ("Choose the correct sentence.", ["À peine il était arrivé que la pluie tomba.", "À peine était-il arrivé que la pluie tomba.", "À peine il est-il arrivé que la pluie tomba.", "À peine était arrivé il que la pluie tomba."], 1,
             "*À peine* at the start of the clause takes inversion: *était-il arrivé* (*était* + *il* joined by a hyphen) followed by *que*.",
             hint="Where must the pronoun go in relation to the auxiliary?",
             feedback=["Without inversion the sentence is not formal French.", "", "The auxiliary and the pronoun are repeated incorrectly.", "The pronoun must follow the auxiliary directly, before the participle."]),
        PQ("Rewrite with inversion: (a) *Sans doute elle a oublié.* (b) *Ainsi la réunion s'acheva.* (c) *« Je ne sais pas », il a répondu.*",
           "(a) **Sans doute a-t-elle oublié.** (b) **Ainsi s'acheva la réunion.** (c) **« Je ne sais pas », a-t-il répondu.** (The *-t-* is needed because *a* ends in a vowel.) Dialogue tags such as *dit-il, répondit-elle* invert in French narration."),
        PQ("Complete with inversion: *À peine le professeur ... (entrer) que les élèves se levèrent.*",
           "**À peine le professeur était-il entré que les élèves se levèrent.** Complex inversion: the noun *le professeur* stays in front and *il* follows *était*."),
        PQ("Write two sentences of description using place-first inversion. Topic: a market in the early morning.",
           "Model: *Sous les bâches bleues s'alignaient les étals de tomates. Autour des vendeuses résonnaient les appels des clients.* The subject (*les étals, les appels*) comes after the verb (*s'alignaient, résonnaient*)."),
        SUM("Simple inversion: verb + hyphen + pronoun; complex inversion: noun subject + verb + hyphen + pronoun; add *-t-* after a vowel before *il, elle, on*.",
            "Use it after *peut-être, sans doute, à peine, ainsi, aussi, en vain, du moins*, after quotations (*dit-il*), in relative clauses and after a place complement.",
            "*Aussi* at the head of a clause means *therefore* and triggers inversion.",
            "Recognise literary forms (*Puissiez-vous, Fût-il*); use inversion sparingly in your own writing."),
    ],
))

# ================================================================== 6. Stylistique comparée
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Stylistique comparée anglais-français",
    title="Stylistique comparée : les procédés de traduction",
    slug="frl1-stylistique-comparee",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Translation is not word-for-word substitution: learn Vinay and Darbelnet's seven procedures (borrowing, calque, literal translation, transposition, modulation, equivalence, adaptation) and the main habits that separate English from French.",
    objectives=["Name the seven translation procedures and give an example of each", "Use transposition and modulation to produce natural French or English", "Spot the main contrasts in word order, verbs of motion, noun groups and prepositions", "Avoid common false friends (faux amis)"],
    key_concepts=["Emprunt, calque, traduction littérale", "Transposition", "Modulation", "Équivalence", "Adaptation", "Faux amis"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Cameroon is officially bilingual, so many students already translate in their heads every day. But a good translation is not a copy of the words; it is a **new text** that a native reader would accept as natural. The study of how English and French express the same idea in different ways is called **stylistique comparée** (comparative stylistics). The classic account is the book by the Canadian linguists Jean-Paul Vinay and Jean Darbelnet (1958), whose seven procedures are the basis of many French courses."),
        H("The seven procedures"),
        D("Vinay and Darbelnet distinguish **direct translation** (three procedures, where the two languages are close) and **oblique translation** (four procedures, used when a literal rendering would be wrong, strange or impossible).", title="Definition"),
        KEY("Direct procedures", "Use them when the language has the same idea and the same structure.", cards=[
            ["Emprunt (borrowing)", "The word is taken over unchanged: *le week-end, le football, un sandwich*."],
            ["Calque", "A foreign expression is translated part by part: *gratte-ciel* (skyscraper)."],
            ["Traduction littérale", "Word for word, with correct grammar: *J'aime ce livre* = *I like this book*."]]),
        KEY("Oblique procedures", "Use them when the direct way sounds unnatural.", cards=[
            ["Transposition", "A word changes its grammatical class, with the same meaning: *As soon as he gets back* > *Dès son retour* (clause to noun)."],
            ["Modulation", "The point of view or the image changes: *It is not difficult to show that ...* > *Il est facile de montrer que ...*."],
            ["Équivalence", "A different expression for the same situation: *Wet paint* > *Peinture fraîche*; *Pleased to meet you* > *Enchanté*."],
            ["Adaptation", "The cultural reference is replaced: the letter opening *Dear Sir* > *Monsieur,* and the ending *Yours faithfully* > *Veuillez agréer, Monsieur, l'expression de mes salutations distinguées.*"]]),
        IMG("alfrl1-procedes-traduction.svg", alt="Diagram of the seven translation procedures arranged from the most literal (borrowing) to the most free (adaptation).", caption="Figure 1. The seven procedures, from closest to the original to freest."),
        H("Transposition in practice"),
        T("English likes verbs and verb particles; French likes nouns and the verb of direction. The commonest transposition is the **manner verb** in English, which becomes a **direction verb** in French, with the manner added at the end. *He swam across the river* becomes *Il a traversé la rivière à la nage*. *She ran into the room* becomes *Elle est entrée dans la pièce en courant.* *He limped away* becomes *Il s'éloigna en boitant.*"),
        T("Other usual transpositions: verb to noun (*After he left > Après son départ*); adjective to adverb (*He is a fast reader > Il lit vite*); noun to verb (*She is a good singer > Elle chante bien*). The key rule is: **keep the meaning, change the form.**"),
        H("Modulation in practice"),
        T("Modulation changes the viewpoint: negative to positive (*Don't forget to lock the door > Pensez à fermer la porte à clé*), cause to effect, part to whole, active to passive. It is chosen when the literal form would be correct grammar but unnatural style."),
        EX("Worked example 1: identify the procedure", intro="Name the procedure: (a) *Beware of the dog* > *Chien méchant.* (b) *She ran into the room* > *Elle est entrée dans la pièce en courant.* (c) *week-end* in French. (d) *It is not difficult to understand* > *Il est facile de comprendre.*", steps=[
            S("(a)", text="A fixed notice with a different French wording: **équivalence**."),
            S("(b)", text="The English verb of manner (*ran*) becomes a direction verb (*entrer*) with the manner added (*en courant*): a **transposition**."),
            S("(c)", text="The English word is borrowed unchanged: **emprunt**."),
            S("(d)", text="A negative statement becomes a positive one: **modulation**."),
        ]),
        H("Habits of the two languages"),
        KEY("Four contrasts that cause errors", "These are tendencies, not absolute rules. Always check the context.", cards=[
            ["Noun groups", "English piles up nouns: *a school bus, the tax reform proposal*. French uses *de* or an adjective: *un car scolaire, la proposition de réforme fiscale, un mur de pierre*."],
            ["Adjective order", "English puts adjectives before the noun. French usually places them after: *une voiture rouge*; a few common short adjectives go before: *une grande maison, un bon élève*."],
            ["-ing forms", "English uses *-ing* freely. French uses the infinitive, a noun or a participle: *Walking is good > Marcher est bon pour la santé*; *Before leaving > Avant de partir*; *On arriving > En arrivant*."],
            ["Prepositions and articles", "French uses articles with abstract nouns and general statements (*La vie est chère*), no article with professions (*Il est médecin*) and different prepositions (*au Cameroun, en France, à Douala*)."]]),
        T("**Spelling and layout.** French writes days, months, languages and nationality adjectives in small letters (*lundi, mars, le français, une ville camerounaise*); the noun for a person is capitalised (*un Camerounais*). Quotation marks are *« »*. A space is placed before *? ! : ;*. Numbers use a space for thousands and a comma for decimals: *1 500,50*. Dates: *le 5 mars 2025*."),
        H("Faux amis"),
        T("Some words look alike but mean different things. Learn the pairs: *actually* means *en fait* (not *actuellement*, which means *currently*); *eventually* means *finalement* (not *éventuellement*, which means *possibly*); *library* is *bibliothèque* (not *librairie*, a bookshop); *to attend* is *assister à* (not *attendre*, which means *to wait for*); English *sensible* is *raisonnable*, while French *sensible* means *sensitive*; *to demand* is *exiger* (not *demander*, which means *to ask*); *to rest* is *se reposer* (not *rester*, which means *to stay*)."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a literal version and a better one", intro="Improve this literal French for *She walked into the room and sat down quietly after the meeting had started*.", steps=[
            S("Literal attempt", text="*Elle a marché dans la pièce et s'est assise tranquillement après que la réunion avait commencé.* The grammar is almost right but the style is not French, and *après que ... avait commencé* is not the right tense."),
            S("Transposition of the verb of manner", text="*Walked into* becomes *entra* (direction verb): *Elle entra dans la pièce.* The manner (*walked*) is not essential here and is dropped."),
            S("Nominalisation of the clause", text="*After the meeting had started* becomes *Après le début de la réunion* (transposition: clause to noun phrase)."),
            S("Result", text="**Elle entra dans la pièce et s'assit discrètement après le début de la réunion.** *Discrètement* renders *quietly* in this context (modulation of the image)."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Translating word by word", "A sentence can have correct French words in the wrong order or with the wrong prepositions. Read the English for the sense first, then ask what a French writer would say in that situation."),
        MISTAKE("Falling for a false friend", "*Il a assisté son ami* means he helped his friend, not that he attended. *Le professeur est sensible* means *sensitive*. Keep a list of false friends and check them in every translation."),
        MISTAKE("Copying English punctuation or capitals", "Do not capitalise *lundi, janvier, français* (language), or write *1,500* for fifteen hundred. Do not forget the space before *?* and *!* in a typed answer."),
        QUIZ("Which procedure turns *As soon as he arrives* into *Dès son arrivée* ?", ["Emprunt", "Calque", "Transposition", "Adaptation"], 2,
             "The clause becomes a noun phrase: a change of grammatical class with the same meaning. That is **transposition**.",
             hint="Does the form change while the meaning stays the same?",
             feedback=["A borrowing keeps the foreign word unchanged.", "A calque translates the parts one by one.", "", "Adaptation replaces a cultural reference."]),
        QUIZ("Which French sentence best translates *He ran across the street*?", ["Il a couru à travers la rue.", "Il a traversé la rue en courant.", "Il a traversé courir la rue.", "Il courait de l'autre côté."], 1,
             "French uses the direction verb *traverser* with *en courant* for the manner.",
             hint="Which verb should carry the direction?",
             feedback=["Correct grammar, but English-style: French prefers the direction verb.", "", "*Courir* cannot follow *traversé* in this way.", "This describes an imperfect action and changes the meaning."]),
        PQ("Name the procedure and give the French. (a) *Keep off the grass* (notice). (b) *to take a decision.* (c) *The meeting begins at 9.*",
           "(a) **Équivalence**: *Pelouse interdite.* (b) **Traduction littérale** works: *prendre une décision.* (Check collocations: French does not say *faire une décision*.) (c) *La réunion commence à neuf heures.* is also a literal translation. These procedures are all direct except (a)."),
        PQ("Translate, using transposition: *He answered without thinking.* and *The government's refusal surprised everyone.*",
           "*Il a répondu sans réfléchir.* (verb phrase with *sans* + infinitive). *Le refus du gouvernement a surpris tout le monde.* (noun to noun; if you prefer a verb: *Le gouvernement a refusé et cela a surpris tout le monde.*). The first translation uses the infinitive after *sans*, which replaces the English *-ing*."),
        PQ("Give the French for these false friends: *He is currently studying.* *Eventually, they agreed.* *The library is open.*",
           "*Il étudie actuellement.* *Finalement, ils ont accepté.* *La bibliothèque est ouverte.* Note that *éventuellement* means *possibly* and *librairie* means *bookshop*."),
        TIP("Paper 3 asks for translation both into and out of French. In your answer for the examiner, a natural sentence that keeps the meaning earns more than an awkward word-for-word copy. Do not leave a gap: if a word is unknown, translate the sense."),
        SUM("Vinay and Darbelnet describe seven procedures: borrowing, calque, literal translation (direct) and transposition, modulation, equivalence, adaptation (oblique).",
            "English manner verbs often become French direction verbs with the manner added: *traverser en courant*.",
            "Watch noun groups, adjective position, *-ing* forms, prepositions, articles, capitals and number layout.",
            "Learn false friends: *actuellement, éventuellement, librairie, assister, sensible, demander*."),
    ],
))

# ================================================================== 7. Traduire les temps et le passif
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Traduire les temps et le passif",
    title="Traduire les temps et le passif : de l'anglais au français",
    slug="frl1-traduire-temps-passif",
    minutes=24,
    summary="English tenses and passives do not map one to one onto French: learn how depuis, venir de, the imperfect, the future in time clauses and the many ways of avoiding the passive (on, pronominal, active) work in translation.",
    objectives=["Translate English perfect and continuous tenses using depuis, venir de and the imperfect", "Translate would, used to and could correctly", "Use the future and the conditional where French requires them after quand, lorsque and si", "Avoid English-style passives by using on, a pronominal verb or a different subject"],
    key_concepts=["Depuis + présent / imparfait", "Venir de", "Would / used to / could", "Futur après quand", "Éviter le passif"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("English has about a dozen verb forms (*I have been waiting, I was waiting, I used to wait, I would wait*). French has fewer, and the same English form can need different French tenses. So the first rule of translation is: **translate the meaning of the tense, not the tense itself.**"),
        H("Perfect and continuous tenses"),
        KEY("Since, for and just", "English perfect tenses with *since / for* describe a situation that is still true. French uses the **present** (or the imperfect in the past) with *depuis*.", cards=[
            ["Present perfect + since/for", "*I have lived here for five years* > *J'habite ici depuis cinq ans.* *She has been working since eight o'clock* > *Elle travaille depuis huit heures.*"],
            ["Past perfect + since/for", "*I had been waiting for an hour when she arrived* > *J'attendais depuis une heure quand elle est arrivée.*"],
            ["Just", "*He has just left* > *Il vient de partir.* *He had just left* > *Il venait de partir.*"],
            ["Completed action (no since/for)", "*I have finished* > *J'ai fini.* *I have never seen it* > *Je ne l'ai jamais vu.*"],
            ["Question", "*How long have you been learning French?* > *Depuis combien de temps apprends-tu le français ?*"]]),
        T("The **simple past** of English is translated by the passé composé in conversation, letters and essays: *He arrived yesterday > Il est arrivé hier.* In formal narrative (stories, history) the **passé simple** is used: *Il arriva le lendemain.* The imperfect describes a situation or a repeated action: *Il pleuvait. Chaque matin, elle allait au marché.*"),
        H("Would, used to, could"),
        T("**Would** has three meanings. (1) Hypothetical: *I would come if I could* > *Je viendrais si je pouvais* (conditional). (2) Repeated habit in the past: *Every Sunday he would visit his aunt* > *Chaque dimanche, il rendait visite à sa tante* (imperfect). (3) Future in the past: *He said he would come* > *Il a dit qu'il viendrait* (conditional)."),
        T("**Used to** is the imperfect: *He used to play football* > *Il jouait au football.* **Could** is *pouvoir* for ability in general (imperfect *pouvait* or conditional *pourrait*), but for something managed **once** use the passé composé: *He could not open the door* > *Il n'a pas pu ouvrir la porte.* For a learnt skill use *savoir*: *She could swim at five > Elle savait nager à cinq ans.*"),
        H("Future and conditional in subordinate clauses"),
        T("In English the present replaces the future after *when, as soon as, after*: *When you arrive, call me.* French keeps the **future**: *Quand tu arriveras, appelle-moi.* *As soon as he gets home, he will cook > Dès qu'il rentrera, il fera la cuisine.* But after *si* (*if*), the present stays: *If it rains tomorrow > S'il pleut demain.*"),
        EX("Worked example 1: tenses", intro="Translate: (a) *We have been studying French for three years.* (b) *She had just finished when the phone rang.* (c) *When the rain stops, we will leave.*", steps=[
            S("(a)", text="Still true now: present + *depuis*: **Nous étudions le français depuis trois ans.**"),
            S("(b)", text="Past perfect + *just*: *venir de* in the imperfect; *rang* is the passé composé: **Elle venait de finir quand le téléphone a sonné.**"),
            S("(c)", text="Future after *quand*: **Quand la pluie s'arrêtera, nous partirons.**"),
        ]),
        H("The passive"),
        T("English uses the passive constantly (*English is spoken here; he was given a book; I was told*). French uses the true passive (*être* + participle) more rarely. The English-style passive is often replaced by one of three strategies."),
        KEY("Four ways to translate a passive", "The English passive may not be the best structure in French.", cards=[
            ["On + active verb", "*English is spoken here > Ici on parle anglais.* *I was told that ... > On m'a dit que ...* *He was seen leaving > On l'a vu partir.*"],
            ["Pronominal verb", "*This product sells well > Ce produit se vend bien.* *This word is no longer used > Ce mot ne s'emploie plus.* *That is not done > Cela ne se fait pas.*"],
            ["Active verb with a new subject", "*The road is being repaired > On répare la route* or *La route est en réparation.* *She was surprised by the news > La nouvelle l'a surprise.*"],
            ["True passive", "Kept when the agent matters or the action is the focus: *La loi a été votée par l'Assemblée.* *Les résultats seront publiés vendredi.*"]]),
        T("**An English indirect-object passive cannot be copied.** *He was given a prize* is not *Il a été donné un prix.* French uses *On lui a remis un prix.* Likewise *She was told to wait > On lui a dit d'attendre.* Only the **direct** object of the active sentence can become the subject of a French passive."),
        EX("Worked example 2: avoiding the passive", intro="Translate: (a) *The village was founded in 1920.* (b) *Cassava is eaten in many regions.* (c) *We were asked to wait.*", steps=[
            S("(a) A history statement", text="The true passive is natural, with a date: **Le village a été fondé en 1920.** (Or the literary *fut fondé*.)"),
            S("(b) A general statement", text="A pronominal verb or *on*: **Le manioc se mange dans de nombreuses régions** or **On mange le manioc dans de nombreuses régions.**"),
            S("(c) Indirect object", text="*Demander à quelqu'un de* uses an indirect object, so no true passive: **On nous a demandé d'attendre.**"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using the present perfect literally", "*J'ai vécu ici pendant cinq ans* is possible only if the person has stopped living there. For a situation that continues, use the present with *depuis*."),
        MISTAKE("Using the present after *quand*", "*Quand il arrive, j'appellerai* is wrong. French requires the future in both clauses: *Quand il arrivera, je l'appellerai.*"),
        MISTAKE("Translating every English passive with *être*", "*Il a été donné un livre* and *Il a été dit que ...* are weak. Use *on* or restructure."),
        TIP("In a translation, ask three questions of every verb: What is the time of the action (now? before? habit?). Is it a completed fact or a continuing situation? Does the subject matter, or can I use *on*?"),
        QUIZ("How do we translate *I have lived in Douala since 2018*?", ["J'ai vécu à Douala depuis 2018.", "Je vis à Douala depuis 2018.", "Je vécus à Douala depuis 2018.", "J'avais vécu à Douala depuis 2018."], 1,
             "The situation continues, so the present with *depuis*: *Je vis à Douala depuis 2018.* (*J'habite* is equally correct.)",
             hint="Is the person still living there?",
             feedback=["*Depuis* with the passé composé is wrong in this sense.", "", "The passé simple is a literary past tense and cannot go with *depuis* here.", "The pluperfect would put the situation before another past time."]),
        QUIZ("How do we translate *He was given a prize*?", ["Il a été donné un prix.", "Il a été donné un prix par le directeur.", "On lui a remis un prix.", "Un prix a donné lui."], 2,
             "*Remettre un prix à quelqu'un* takes an indirect object (*lui*), so the English passive is replaced by *on*.",
             hint="Who is the person in the French sentence: the subject or the indirect object?",
             feedback=["The indirect object cannot become a French passive subject.", "Same error with the agent added.", "", "The word order and the grammar are wrong."]),
        PQ("Translate into French: (a) *She has worked here since 2020.* (b) *He used to walk to school.* (c) *If you come tomorrow, we will visit the market.*",
           "(a) **Elle travaille ici depuis 2020.** (b) **Il allait à l'école à pied.** (imperfect of habit) (c) **Si tu viens demain, nous visiterons le marché.** (present after *si*, future in the result)"),
        PQ("Translate avoiding an English-style passive: (a) *French is spoken in many African countries.* (b) *I was told the shop was closed.*",
           "(a) **On parle français dans de nombreux pays africains.** or **Le français se parle dans de nombreux pays africains.** (b) **On m'a dit que la boutique était fermée.**"),
        PQ("Translate: *He said he would call me when he got home.*",
           "**Il a dit qu'il m'appellerait quand il rentrerait.** *Would* in the reported speech is the conditional (future in the past), and *quand* is followed by the same tense (conditional of *rentrer*, for the future in the past)."),
        SUM("Use the present (imperfect in the past) with *depuis* for a situation still true; *venir de* translates *just*.",
            "*Would* may be conditional, imperfect of habit or future in the past; *could* once = passé composé of *pouvoir*, ability = *savoir*.",
            "After *quand, lorsque, dès que* use the future (or conditional in reported speech); after *si* use the present.",
            "Avoid the English passive: use *on*, a pronominal verb or a different subject; indirect objects never become the subject of a French passive."),
    ],
))

# ================================================================== 8. Traduire les expressions idiomatiques
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Traduire les expressions idiomatiques",
    title="Traduire les expressions idiomatiques et les proverbes",
    slug="frl1-expressions-idiomatiques",
    minutes=22,
    summary="An idiom must be translated by its meaning, not its words: learn to recognise idioms, find the French equivalent (same image, different image, or no image) and keep the register right.",
    objectives=["Recognise that an expression is an idiom and refuse a word-for-word rendering", "Translate common idioms and proverbs in both directions", "Choose between an equivalent idiom and a plain explanation", "Respect register and collocation"],
    key_concepts=["Expression idiomatique", "Image identique / différente / aucune", "Proverbe", "Registre", "Collocation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A candidate who translates *Il pleut des cordes* as *It rains ropes* shows that the idiom was not recognised, and an English reader will be puzzled. Idioms are fixed expressions whose meaning is not the sum of their words. Translating them well is one of the most visible signs of skill in Paper 3 and in vocabulary items of Paper 1."),
        D("An **expression idiomatique** is a group of words whose meaning is figurative or conventional and cannot be deduced word by word. A **proverbe** is a short, well-known sentence that expresses popular wisdom. A **collocation** is a habitual combination of words (*prendre une décision*), not figurative but fixed.", title="Definition"),
        H("Three situations"),
        KEY("Which situation are you in?", "When you meet an idiom, ask whether the other language has the same image, a different image or none at all.", cards=[
            ["Same image", "*Prendre le taureau par les cornes* = *to take the bull by the horns.* *Une pierre qui roule n'amasse pas mousse* = *a rolling stone gathers no moss.* Translate literally."],
            ["Different image", "*Il pleut des cordes* = *It is raining cats and dogs* (in French: ropes). *Faire d'une pierre deux coups* = *to kill two birds with one stone.*"],
            ["No idiom in the target", "Explain the meaning plainly: *poser un lapin à quelqu'un* = *to stand someone up.*"]]),
        IMG("alfrl1-idiomes.svg", alt="Decision diagram: is it an idiom? Look for an equivalent with the same image, a different image, or translate the meaning in plain words.", caption="Figure 1. How to translate an idiom."),
        H("A bank of useful pairs"),
        KEY("French to English", "These are standard and widely listed; check your dictionary for register.", cards=[
            ["Il pleut des cordes.", "It is pouring (raining cats and dogs)."],
            ["Faire d'une pierre deux coups.", "To kill two birds with one stone."],
            ["Vendre la mèche.", "To let the cat out of the bag."],
            ["Être aux anges.", "To be over the moon."],
            ["Coûter les yeux de la tête.", "To cost an arm and a leg."],
            ["Tourner autour du pot.", "To beat around the bush."],
            ["Se lever du pied gauche.", "To get out of bed on the wrong side."],
            ["Retourner à la case départ.", "To go back to square one."],
            ["Quand les poules auront des dents.", "When pigs fly."],
            ["Avoir un chat dans la gorge.", "To have a frog in one's throat."],
            ["Ce n'est pas la mer à boire.", "It's not that difficult."],
            ["Appeler un chat un chat.", "To call a spade a spade."]]),
        KEY("Proverbs", "Proverbs are usually translated by the equivalent proverb.", cards=[
            ["Mieux vaut tard que jamais.", "Better late than never."],
            ["L'habit ne fait pas le moine.", "Don't judge a book by its cover."],
            ["Chat échaudé craint l'eau froide.", "Once bitten, twice shy."],
            ["Mieux vaut prévenir que guérir.", "Prevention is better than cure."],
            ["Les murs ont des oreilles.", "Walls have ears."],
            ["Rome ne s'est pas faite en un jour.", "Rome was not built in a day."]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: translate into English", intro="Translate: *Quand il a appris la nouvelle, il était aux anges, mais il a vendu la mèche trop tôt.*", steps=[
            S("Recognise the idioms", text="*Être aux anges* and *vendre la mèche* are idioms."),
            S("Find the equivalents", text="*Être aux anges* = *to be over the moon*; *vendre la mèche* = *to let the cat out of the bag* (or *to give the game away*)."),
            S("Assemble", text="**When he heard the news he was over the moon, but he let the cat out of the bag too soon.**"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: translate into French", intro="Translate: *The repairs cost an arm and a leg, so we are back to square one.*", steps=[
            S("Idioms", text="*Cost an arm and a leg* > *coûter les yeux de la tête*; *back to square one* > *retourner à la case départ*."),
            S("Grammar", text="*We are back* can be rendered *nous voilà revenus*, a natural French way of saying *here we are again*."),
            S("Assemble", text="**Les réparations coûtent les yeux de la tête, alors nous voilà revenus à la case départ.**"),
        ]),
        H("Collocations and register"),
        T("Even without idioms, English and French combine words differently. English *make a mistake* is *faire une faute* (or *une erreur*). *Take an exam* is *passer un examen*; *do homework* is *faire ses devoirs*; *pay attention* is *faire attention*; *catch a cold* is *attraper un rhume*; *have lunch* is *déjeuner*; *make progress* is *faire des progrès*. Use the dictionary example sentences to find the right verb."),
        T("**Register.** *C'est du gâteau* (it's a piece of cake) is informal and fits dialogue, not a formal essay. *Casser les pieds à quelqu'un* is informal. A formal text would use *C'est très facile* or *Cela dérange quelqu'un*. Match the register of your translation to the source."),
        MISTAKE("Word-for-word idioms", "*Il pleut des cordes* is not *It rains ropes*. Do not translate the image; translate the meaning."),
        MISTAKE("Using an idiom that does not exist", "Do not invent a French idiom by translating an English one (*Il pleut chats et chiens* is wrong). Find the real equivalent or use plain words."),
        MISTAKE("Wrong register", "Do not use an informal idiom in a formal passage or a stiff expression in a friendly dialogue."),
        QUIZ("What is the best English translation of *Il a posé un lapin à son ami* ?", ["He put a rabbit to his friend.", "He stood his friend up.", "He asked his friend a question.", "He gave his friend a rabbit."], 1,
             "*Poser un lapin à quelqu'un* means to fail to turn up for an appointment: *to stand someone up*.",
             hint="Think of the situation: a meeting where one person is absent.",
             feedback=["Word for word: it makes no sense.", "", "*Poser* does not mean *ask* in this expression.", "No gift is involved."]),
        QUIZ("Which is the French equivalent of *Better late than never* ?", ["Mieux vaut jamais que tard.", "Mieux vaut tard que jamais.", "Tard vaut mieux que jamais.", "Jamais vaut mieux que tard."], 1,
             "The standard proverb is *Mieux vaut tard que jamais.*",
             hint="Start from the same words as *Mieux vaut prévenir que guérir*.",
             feedback=["The meaning is reversed.", "", "Not the idiom: the verb *valoir* is missing.", "Not an idiom."]),
        PQ("Translate into English: (a) *Il faut appeler un chat un chat.* (b) *Il a fait d'une pierre deux coups en réglant ses affaires à Douala.*",
           "(a) **We must call a spade a spade.** (b) **He killed two birds with one stone by settling his business in Douala.** Keep the idiom in the same register as the original."),
        PQ("Translate into French: (a) *He let the cat out of the bag.* (b) *It was pouring when we left.* (c) *The price is out of my reach and costs an arm and a leg.*",
           "(a) **Il a vendu la mèche.** (b) **Il pleuvait à verse quand nous sommes partis.** (also *Il pleuvait des cordes*) (c) **Ce prix est hors de ma portée : cela coûte les yeux de la tête.**"),
        PQ("Explain in a sentence in English why *Il avait un chat dans la gorge* cannot be translated word for word, and give a natural translation.",
           "The French idiom means that his voice was hoarse or blocked, not that an animal was in his throat. A natural translation is **He had a frog in his throat.** (The image is different in each language but the meaning is the same.)"),
        TIP("In an exam passage, circle every phrase that sounds odd when read word for word. If you recognise an idiom, use the equivalent; if you do not, translate the sense in plain, natural words instead of leaving a literal image."),
        SUM("An idiom is translated by its meaning, never word for word.",
            "Check whether the other language has the same image, a different image or none; in the last case, explain plainly.",
            "Proverbs have equivalent proverbs: learn the common pairs.",
            "Respect collocations (*faire une faute, prendre une décision*) and register."),
    ],
))

# ================================================================== 9. Le thème
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Le thème",
    title="Le thème : traduire de l'anglais vers le français",
    slug="frl1-theme",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Thème is translation into French: read the whole text, mark the traps, translate by units of meaning, then check agreements, tenses, articles, prepositions and false friends.",
    objectives=["Apply a six-step method to a passage from English into French", "Avoid the typical English-to-French errors (articles, prepositions, tenses, idioms, nouns)", "Check the draft with a short list of grammar points", "Translate a short passage and correct a faulty translation"],
    key_concepts=["Méthode du thème", "Unités de sens", "Pièges grammaticaux", "Relecture", "Registre"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("The **thème** is the translation of an English passage into French. In Paper 3 the translation is part of the paper on essay writing and translation. The Board's report for 2023 describes the translation performance as acceptable and puts it down to vocabulary within the candidates' reach, simple sentence structures and passages drawn from life situations; it adds that translating from French into English helped. For the essay questions, the report names language as the main difficulty: vocabulary, grammar and the construction of sentences. Thème therefore trains the exact skills that the examiner is looking for."),
        T("Examiners judge **accuracy** (is the meaning complete and correct?) and **French** (grammar, spelling, vocabulary, style). Do not leave words out and do not add ideas."),
        H("A six-step method"),
        KEY("Method for a thème", "Follow the steps in order; do not start writing before step 3.", cards=[
            ["1. Read the whole text", "Understand the subject, the register and the tenses. Never translate a sentence before knowing the context."],
            ["2. Mark the traps", "Underline idioms, false friends, passives, *-ing* forms, perfect tenses, *would/could*, modal verbs, quantifiers, dates and numbers."],
            ["3. Translate by units of meaning", "Translate ideas, not words. Use transposition and modulation if needed (see the lesson on stylistique comparée)."],
            ["4. Choose the tense", "Decide whether each verb is a fact, a habit, a hypothesis, a continuing situation. Match the sequence of tenses."],
            ["5. Check the grammar", "Genders, adjective agreement, past participle agreement, articles, prepositions, accents, verb endings."],
            ["6. Reread against the English", "Check that nothing is omitted or added. Read the French aloud in your head: does it sound French?"]]),
        H("The usual traps"),
        T("**Articles and professions.** *He is a doctor* > *Il est médecin* (no article after *être* with a profession). *She is a nurse and she is thirty* > *Elle est infirmière et elle a trente ans* (age uses *avoir*). **Feelings and weather.** *It is cold* > *Il fait froid*; *I am cold* > *J'ai froid*; *we are hungry* > *nous avons faim*."),
        T("**Countable and uncountable nouns.** English singulars like *news, advice, information, furniture, progress* are often plural or different in French: *The news was good > Les nouvelles étaient bonnes*; *She gave me advice > Elle m'a donné des conseils*; *Information is useful > Les informations sont utiles*; *They made progress > Ils ont fait des progrès*. **People.** *Most people prefer ...* > *La plupart des gens préfèrent ...*; *The people of Cameroon > Les Camerounais.*"),
        T("**Prepositions with places.** *in Cameroon > au Cameroun*; *in France > en France*; *in Douala > à Douala*; *from Cameroon > du Cameroun*. **Days.** *on Monday > lundi* (no article); *on Mondays > le lundi.* **Numbers.** *3.5 > 3,5*; *1,500 > 1 500*."),
        EX("Worked example 1: sentence-level traps", intro="Translate: (a) *My brother is a mechanic and he is twenty-five.* (b) *The weather is hot, so we are thirsty.* (c) *I have been learning French for three years.*", steps=[
            S("(a)", text="No article before the profession; age with *avoir*: **Mon frère est mécanicien et il a vingt-cinq ans.**"),
            S("(b)", text="*Il fait chaud* for the weather; *avoir soif* for the feeling: **Il fait chaud, alors nous avons soif.**"),
            S("(c)", text="Continuing situation: **J'apprends le français depuis trois ans.**"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a short passage", intro="Translate the passage into French. *Many families in our towns now use electric cooking equipment. Although it makes daily life easier, the cost of electricity has risen and power cuts are frequent. If the government invested more in power stations, the situation would improve. Last year my aunt, who lives in Bafoussam, bought a refrigerator which she had wanted for a long time.*", steps=[
            S("Steps 1 and 2: read and mark", text="Traps: *Many families* (agreement), *although* (subjunctive), *has risen* (still true: passé composé fits), *power cuts* (fixed phrase), *if ... invested ... would improve* (si + imparfait, conditional), *who lives* (relative), *had wanted* with a duration."),
            S("Steps 3 and 4: translate and choose tenses", text="*Beaucoup de familles de nos villes utilisent désormais des appareils de cuisine électriques. Bien que cela facilite la vie quotidienne, le coût de l'électricité a augmenté et les coupures de courant sont fréquentes.*"),
            S("Continue", text="*Si le gouvernement investissait davantage dans les centrales électriques, la situation s'améliorerait. L'année dernière, ma tante, qui habite à Bafoussam, a acheté un réfrigérateur dont elle avait envie depuis longtemps.*"),
            S("Step 5: check", text="*Bien que* + subjunctive (*facilite*, same form as the indicative for *-er* verbs). *Si* + imparfait + conditional. *Dont* for *avoir envie de*. *À Bafoussam* (town). Past participle *acheté* does not agree (the object *un réfrigérateur* follows)."),
        ]),
        T("Notice the choices. *Make daily life easier* became *faciliter la vie quotidienne* (a stronger verb in French). *Now* became *désormais*. *Power cuts* is *coupures de courant*. A different but correct answer is possible: for example *Bien qu'ils rendent la vie quotidienne plus facile ...* The examiner will accept different good solutions as long as the meaning is complete and the French is correct."),
        H("Checking your draft"),
        KEY("Last-minute checklist", "Look for these errors in turn.", cards=[
            ["Verbs", "Subject-verb agreement, correct tense and mood after *bien que, si, quand*."],
            ["Nouns and adjectives", "Gender and number: *un problème, une famille*; adjective agreement."],
            ["Participles", "Agreement with *être* and with a preceding direct object."],
            ["Small words", "Articles, prepositions, *de* after quantifiers (*beaucoup de, plusieurs*), accents and apostrophes."]]),
        MISTAKE("Leaving words out", "If you do not know a word, give your best French for the sense. A missing phrase costs more than an imperfect one."),
        MISTAKE("Anglicisms", "*Je suis vingt ans* and *Je suis froid* are errors. So is *Il a fait une décision* (French says *prendre une décision*). Keep checking whether a phrase is really French."),
        MISTAKE("Inconsistent tenses", "Keep the same time frame throughout a passage, switching tense only when the meaning requires it."),
        QUIZ("Which is the correct translation of *He is a teacher and he is thirty years old*?", ["Il est un professeur et il est trente ans.", "Il est professeur et il a trente ans.", "Il a professeur et il est trente ans.", "Il est un professeur et il a trente ans."], 1,
             "Profession after *être* has no article; age uses *avoir*.",
             hint="Which verb for age? Which article for professions?",
             feedback=["Two errors: the article and *être* for age.", "", "*Avoir* cannot be used for the profession.", "The article is wrong."]),
        QUIZ("Which is the best translation of *Most people prefer to travel by road*?", ["La plupart des gens préfèrent voyager par la route.", "Plupart gens préfère voyager sur route.", "Les plus gens préfèrent voyager par la route.", "La majorité des gens préfère voyager route."], 0,
             "*La plupart des* is followed by the plural noun and plural verb.",
             hint="What is the fixed French expression for *most*?",
             feedback=["", "Without article, *des* and the plural verb.", "*Les plus gens* is not French for *most people*.", "Verb in the singular and the preposition missing."]),
        PQ("Translate into French: *My sister, who works in Yaoundé, has just bought a house which she had been dreaming of for years.*",
           "**Ma sœur, qui travaille à Yaoundé, vient d'acheter une maison dont elle rêvait depuis des années.** *Venir de* translates *has just*; *dont* for *rêver de*; imperfect with *depuis* for the duration."),
        PQ("Correct the faulty translation: *Il est un étudiant. Il est vingt ans. Il fait froid parce qu'il est froid.*",
           "**Il est étudiant. Il a vingt ans. Il a froid.** (The last sentence can be turned into *Il fait froid* if it means the weather.) The errors were: article after *être* with a profession, *être* for age, *être froid* for the feeling."),
        PQ("Translate: *If prices fell, more families would buy electric cookers.*",
           "**Si les prix baissaient, davantage de familles achèteraient des cuisinières électriques.** (*Si* + imparfait, conditional; *davantage de* + noun.)"),
        TIP("In the exam, leave time to reread. Write the French clearly: a single wrong accent in a clear script is better than an illegible word. Where the Board's passages concern everyday life, use the simplest correct French rather than a fancy structure you may get wrong."),
        SUM("Use the six-step method: read, mark traps, translate meaning units, choose tenses, check grammar, reread.",
            "Typical traps: articles and professions, age and feelings with *avoir*, plural English singulars, prepositions with places, numbers and dates.",
            "Use *depuis*, *venir de*, *si* + imparfait/conditionnel, *dont*, and the subjunctive after *bien que* where the English suggests them.",
            "Never leave a gap; a close translation of the sense is better than none."),
    ],
))

# ================================================================== 10. La version
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="La version",
    title="La version : traduire du français vers l'anglais",
    slug="frl1-version",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Version is translation into English: understand the French first, find the natural English for each tense, passive, nominal style and false friend, and check that the result reads like English written by an English speaker.",
    objectives=["Apply a method to translate a French passage into natural English", "Translate French tenses (passé simple, imparfait, depuis, venir de) and constructions (on, ne ... que, se faire + infinitive, faillir) correctly", "Convert nominal style and inversion into plain English", "Spot false friends when reading French"],
    key_concepts=["Méthode de la version", "Temps français et équivalents anglais", "Style nominal", "Faux amis (sens français)", "Anglais naturel"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In the **version** you translate from French into English. It seems easier because English is the language you write best, but the risks are different: you can misread a French construction, copy French word order, or leave a false friend. The Board's report suggests that candidates found the translation into English helpful for their results; use that advantage by being precise."),
        T("The examiner wants two things: a **correct understanding** of the French, and **natural English**. A version that is correct word by word but sounds foreign will lose marks."),
        H("A method"),
        KEY("Method for a version", "Understand first, write second.", cards=[
            ["1. Read twice", "Read for the general idea, then for details. Note the tenses and the register."],
            ["2. Find the difficulties", "Underline idioms, false friends, subjunctives, inversions, *on*, *y* and *en*, long sentences."],
            ["3. Translate sentence by sentence", "Look for the English structure that carries the same idea. Do not copy French word order."],
            ["4. Reread as English", "Read your version without the French. Does it read like English? Check spelling, punctuation and tenses."],
            ["5. Compare with the French", "Check that nothing is omitted, nothing is added and no word is left in French."]]),
        H("French tenses into English"),
        KEY("Tense equivalents", "Translate the meaning of the tense.", cards=[
            ["Passé simple", "*Elle ouvrit la porte et vit un étranger.* > *She opened the door and saw a stranger.* (simple past)"],
            ["Imparfait", "Description: *Il pleuvait.* > *It was raining.* Habit: *Il allait au marché le samedi.* > *He used to go / would go to the market on Saturdays.*"],
            ["Depuis + présent", "*Il travaille ici depuis trois ans.* > *He has been working here for three years.*"],
            ["Depuis + imparfait", "*Elle attendait depuis une heure.* > *She had been waiting for an hour.*"],
            ["Venir de", "*Il vient de partir.* > *He has just left.* *Il venait de partir.* > *He had just left.*"],
            ["Futur après quand", "*Quand il arrivera, je lui parlerai.* > *When he arrives, I will speak to him.* (English uses the present)"]]),
        H("Constructions that mislead"),
        T("**On** may be *we, you, people, they* or a passive: *On dit que ...* > *It is said that ...* or *People say that ...*; *On parle anglais ici* > *English is spoken here.* **Ne ... que** means *only*: *Il ne reste que deux jours* > *Only two days remain.* **Se faire + infinitive** is passive or causative: *Il s'est fait voler son téléphone* > *He had his phone stolen.* **Faillir** means *nearly*: *Il a failli tomber* > *He nearly fell.* **Il y a** with a time expression means *ago*: *Il est parti il y a trois jours* > *He left three days ago.* **Cela fait** + time + **que** means *for*: *Cela fait deux ans qu'elle habite ici* > *She has lived here for two years.*"),
        T("**Devoir** has several senses: *Il doit être malade* > *He must be ill* (deduction); *Il doit partir* > *He has to / must leave*; *Il devait partir* > *He was supposed to leave*; *Il aurait dû partir* > *He should have left.* **Falloir:** *Il faut que tu viennes* > *You must come / You need to come.* Choose from the context."),
        H("Nominal style and inversion into English"),
        T("French nominal style becomes verbs in English: *Dès son arrivée, la pluie cessa* > *As soon as he arrived, the rain stopped.* *La rapidité de sa réaction a surpris* > *The speed of his reaction surprised everyone* (or *He reacted so quickly that everyone was surprised*). Stylistic inversion is simply dropped: *Peut-être a-t-il oublié* > *Perhaps he has forgotten.* *Aussi avons-nous décidé de partir* > *So we decided to leave.*"),
        EX("Worked example 1: a short passage", intro="Translate into English: *Dans de nombreuses villes africaines, la croissance rapide de la population oblige les autorités à repenser les transports. Les embouteillages, qu'aggravent les pluies, font perdre chaque jour des heures aux travailleurs. Il faudrait que les municipalités investissent dans des routes plus solides, et que les habitants acceptent de changer leurs habitudes. Ce n'est qu'ainsi que la ville deviendra plus agréable à vivre.*", steps=[
            S("Read and mark", text="Traps: nominal style (*croissance rapide*), inversion in the relative clause (*qu'aggravent les pluies*), *faire perdre*, *il faudrait que* + subjunctive, *c'est ... que* with *ne ... que*, *agréable à vivre*."),
            S("First two sentences", text="*In many African cities, the rapid growth of the population is forcing the authorities to rethink transport. Traffic jams, made worse by the rains, cost workers hours every day.*"),
            S("Third sentence", text="*Il faudrait que ... investissent* is conditional + subjunctive: *Municipalities would need to invest in sturdier roads, and residents would need to agree to change their habits.*"),
            S("Last sentence", text="*Ce n'est qu'ainsi que* means *only in this way*: *Only in this way will the city become a more pleasant place to live.* (English inverts after *only in this way*.)"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: false friends and tenses", intro="Translate: *Le directeur assiste à la réunion actuellement en cours. Cela fait deux heures qu'on attend, et il vient de dire qu'il pourrait éventuellement partir.*", steps=[
            S("False friends", text="*Assister à* = *to attend*; *actuellement* = *currently*; *éventuellement* = *possibly*."),
            S("Time expressions", text="*Cela fait deux heures qu'on attend* = *we have been waiting for two hours*; *il vient de dire* = *he has just said*."),
            S("Assemble", text="**The headmaster is attending the meeting that is currently under way. We have been waiting for two hours, and he has just said that he might possibly leave.** (*On* is rendered *we* because the speaker is among those waiting; check the context.)"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Translating a false friend by the similar English word", "*Actuellement* is not *actually*, *éventuellement* is not *eventually*, *assister* is not *to assist*, *une librairie* is not a library. Check the context before choosing."),
        MISTAKE("Copying French word order or punctuation", "*Will the city become more pleasant?* is natural English; *Becomes more pleasant the city?* is not. Similarly, keep English capitals and decimal points (*1,500* is written *1 500* in French but *1,500* in English)."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing English tenses", "*He has been working here since three years* is wrong. After *for* use a period (*for three years*); after *since* a point in time (*since 2020*)."),
        TIP("When the English comes out clumsy, ask: how would an English writer say this? Change the word class, change the viewpoint or split a long French sentence into two short English ones."),
        QUIZ("What does *Il a failli tomber* mean?", ["He fell.", "He failed to fall.", "He nearly fell.", "He had to fall."], 2,
             "*Faillir* + infinitive means that something nearly happened.",
             hint="Think of the verb *faillir* in *il a failli rater le train*.",
             feedback=["He did not fall.", "*Faillir* does not mean *fail to*.", "", "It does not express obligation."]),
        QUIZ("How do we translate *Cela fait trois semaines qu'il est malade*?", ["It has made three weeks that he is ill.", "He has been ill for three weeks.", "He was ill three weeks ago.", "He will be ill for three weeks."], 1,
             "*Cela fait ... que* + present expresses a situation continuing up to now: *has been ill for three weeks*.",
             hint="Is the illness over?",
             feedback=["Word for word: not English.", "", "That would be *il y a trois semaines*, a single past event.", "The future does not appear in the French."]),
        PQ("Translate into English: *Elle ne l'avait jamais vu avant, mais elle le reconnut tout de suite.*",
           "**She had never seen him before, but she recognised him at once.** *Avait vu* is the past perfect; *reconnut* (passé simple) is the simple past."),
        PQ("Translate into English: *Le directeur se serait fait voler sa voiture hier soir.*",
           "**The headmaster reportedly had his car stolen last night.** or **The headmaster is said to have had his car stolen last night.** (The conditional signals unconfirmed information, so *reportedly* or *is said to* is needed.)"),
        PQ("Translate into English: *Dès l'arrivée des pluies, les routes devinrent impraticables et les marchés manquèrent de tomates.*",
           "**As soon as the rains arrived, the roads became impassable and the markets ran short of tomatoes.** Nominal style (*dès l'arrivée*) becomes a verb clause; the passé simple becomes the simple past."),
        SUM("Read for the whole meaning, mark difficulties, translate sentence by sentence and reread your English as an English reader.",
            "Translate tenses by meaning: passé simple = simple past; imparfait = was -ing / used to; depuis, venir de, cela fait ... que = has been / has just / for.",
            "Convert nominal style and inversion into plain English; *on* may be *we, people* or a passive.",
            "Beware false friends: *actuellement, éventuellement, assister, librairie*."),
    ],
))
